.Dd May 28, 2026
.Dt ORACLED 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm oracled
.Nd Oracle capability-world service manager
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl f Ar conffile
.Op Fl p Ar pidfile
.Op Fl T
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the system authority for the Oracle capability world \(em a thin
layer that claims system resources via the
.Xr cap_rt 4
capability runtime and delegates them to agents by fork/exec'ing
declared programs and passing capability tokens.
.Nm
does not start individual daemons or manage services directly.
It starts
.Em agents ,
each of which is an init system for its own domain.
.Pp
It is started by
.Xr rc 8 .
The standard agent binary is
.Pa /usr/libexec/oracled/agent .
The oracle forks multiple instances of it, each with a different
label and different capability tokens.
Agent manifests are in
.Pa /etc/oracled.d/ ;
each agent reads its own service directory at
.Pa /etc/oracled.d/{label}.d/
to manage native services, app bundles, or containers.
.Pp
During startup,
.Nm
executes the following phases:
.Bl -enum
.It
Load configuration from
.Pa /etc/oracled.conf
(or the path given with
.Fl f ) ,
open syslog, acquire pidfile.
.It
Daemonize (unless foreground mode).
.It
Harden the process via
.Xr procctl 2
(subtree reaper, OOM protection, trace disable, signal-exit logging).
.It
Open
.Pa /dev/cap_rt
and, if configured, claim it via the isolation service so that only
.Nm
can access the device.
.It
Activate the capprotect shield with the flags specified in the
configuration file.
.It
Create a control socket for administrative commands.
.It
Load agent manifests from
.Pa /etc/oracled.d/ ,
sort by dependency order, and launch each agent with
.Xr pdfork 2 .
.It
Enter the event loop, monitoring agents via
.Xr kqueue 2
process descriptors.
.El
.Pp
Administrative commands are sent via
.Xr oraclectl 8 .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-f conffile"
.It Fl d
Run in the foreground and log to standard error as well as syslog.
.It Fl f Ar conffile
Read configuration from
.Ar conffile
instead of
.Pa /etc/oracled.conf .
.It Fl p Ar pidfile
Write the process ID to
.Ar pidfile
instead of the configured pidfile path.
Overrides the
.Va pidfile
setting in the configuration file.
.It Fl T
Test mode.
Run without requiring root, without daemonizing, and without
applying privileged process controls, capability runtime setup,
or the control socket.
Manifests are parsed and sorted but services are not launched.
Intended for automated tests.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Nm
reads its configuration from
.Pa /etc/oracled.conf
in UCL format.
The configuration file is optional \(em if it does not exist,
compiled-in defaults are used.
Syntax errors are fatal.
.Pp
The following keys are supported:
.Bl -tag -width "control_socket_mode"
.It Va pidfile
Path to the pidfile.
Default:
.Pa /var/run/oracled.pid .
.It Va control_socket
Path to the control socket.
Default:
.Pa /var/run/oracled.sock .
.It Va control_socket_mode
File mode for the control socket.
Default: 0700 (root only).
.It Va manifest_dir
Directory containing service manifest files.
Default:
.Pa /etc/oracled.d .
.El
.Ss Integrity
The
.Va integrity
section controls capprotect flags that protect
.Nm
from external interference.
Each key is a boolean (true/false).
.Bl -tag -width "visible"
.It Va ptrace
Block ptrace attach from foreign nonces.
Default: true.
.It Va signal
Block all signals except SIGKILL and SIGCONT from foreign nonces.
Default: false.
Enabling this prevents
.Xr rc 8
from stopping
.Nm
via signals; use
.Xr oraclectl 8
for shutdown instead.
.It Va visible
Hide
.Nm
from
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr top 1 ,
and procfs enumeration.
Default: false.
.It Va wait
Block wait4 from non-parent processes.
Default: true.
.It Va sched
Block scheduler priority and CPU affinity manipulation.
Default: true.
.It Va core
Suppress core dumps to prevent leaking secrets from process memory.
Default: false.
.It Va ktrace
Block passive syscall tracing via
.Xr ktrace 1 .
Default: true.
.El
.Ss Claims
The
.Va claims
section lists resources under oracle control.
Resources listed here are claimed exclusively by
.Nm
at boot via the cap_rt isolation service.
Once claimed, MACF hooks deny access from all other processes,
including root.
.Pa /dev/cap_rt
is always claimed automatically.
.Bl -tag -width "network"
.It Va paths
Array of file and directory paths to claim.
Claiming a file blocks open, stat, chmod, chown, and other
vnode operations.
Claiming a directory blocks lookup into the subtree,
preventing access to all files within.
Default: empty.
.It Va network
Array of network endpoint objects to claim.
Each object has:
.Va port
(integer),
.Va protocol
("tcp" or "udp"),
.Va direction
("bind", "connect", or "any"),
and optionally
.Va domain
("inet6" or "any"; default is IPv4).
Foreign nonces receive
.Er EACCES
on bind or connect to claimed endpoints.
Default: empty.
.It Va system
Array of system operation names to gate via the
.Xr cap_rt_system 4
service.
Once claimed, even root is denied unless holding an authorized
token from
.Nm .
Available operations:
.Dq kldload ,
.Dq kldunload ,
.Dq kldstat ,
.Dq reboot ,
.Dq swapon ,
.Dq swapoff ,
.Dq sysctl ,
.Dq kenv ,
.Dq acct ,
.Dq audit ,
.Dq kenv_read .
Default: empty (no system operations gated).
.El
.Sh AGENT MANIFESTS
.Nm
does not start individual daemons directly.
It starts
.Em agents
\(em each agent is an init system for its own domain.
Agent manifests are UCL files in
.Pa /etc/oracled.d/
(or the directory given by the
.Va manifest_dir
configuration key).
Each
.Pa *.ucl
file declares one agent to fork.
Files are loaded in lexicographic order.
Invalid manifests are logged and skipped.
.Pp
The standard agent binary is
.Pa /usr/libexec/oracled/agent .
The oracle forks multiple instances of it, each with a different
label and set of capability tokens.
Each agent reads its own service configuration from
.Pa /etc/oracled.d/{label}.d/
and manages its own children: forking binaries, dropping
credentials, supervising restarts, and delegating sub-tokens.
.Pp
The oracle reads
.Pa *.ucl
files from the manifest directory and ignores subdirectories.
Agents read their matching
.Pa .d/
subdirectory.
There is no overlap.
.Pp
The following keys are supported in agent manifests:
.Bl -tag -width "capabilities"
.It Va label Pq required
Unique name for the agent.
Used in log messages, dependency references, and to locate the
agent's service directory at
.Pa /etc/oracled.d/{label}.d/ .
.It Va program Pq required
Absolute path to the agent binary.
Typically
.Pa /usr/libexec/oracled/agent
for all agents.
.It Va description
Human-readable description.
.It Va user
Run the agent as this user.
Sets uid, gid, and supplementary groups via
.Xr getpwnam 3 .
The
.Ev HOME
environment variable is set to the user's home directory.
Agents typically run as root so they can set credentials for
their own children; individual services within the agent drop
privileges as needed.
.It Va group
Run the agent with this primary group.
Overrides the group from
.Va user
if both are set.
.It Va jail
Run the agent inside a jail with this name.
If omitted, the agent runs in jid0 (the host context).
The oracle manages jail creation and JID assignment;
agents do not choose their own JID.
JIDs 0\(en2 are reserved for oracle infrastructure.
User jails start at JID 3.
.Pp
Jail support is not yet implemented.
If set, a warning is logged and the agent runs in jid0.
.It Va provides
Array of capability names this agent provides.
Used by the dependency graph to determine start order.
Other agents reference these names in their
.Va requires
arrays.
.It Va requires
Array of capability names this agent depends on.
The agent will not start until all required capabilities are
available.
The special name
.Dq ORACLED
is always satisfied and refers to
.Nm
itself being ready.
Unknown providers are warned but treated as satisfied.
.It Va restart
Restart policy when the agent exits.
One of:
.Dq never
(default),
.Dq always ,
or
.Dq on-failure
(restart only on non-zero exit).
Rapid restarts (agent dies within 5 seconds) are delayed
with exponential backoff up to 30 seconds.
.It Va capabilities
Object containing capability tokens to delegate to the agent:
.Bl -tag -width "network"
.It Va paths
Array of absolute paths.
.Nm
mints an isolation access token for each path and passes it
to the agent.
The agent must call
.Dv FI_OP_AUTHORIZE
on each token after exec to activate access.
The agent may further delegate these to its children.
.It Va network
Array of network endpoint objects to delegate.
Each object has the same format as
.Va claims.network
in the configuration file:
.Va port
(integer),
.Va protocol
("tcp" or "udp"),
.Va direction
("bind", "connect", or "any"),
and optionally
.Va domain
("inet6" or "any"; default is IPv4).
.Nm
mints a network isolation token covering the oracle's claimed
endpoints and passes it to the agent.
.It Va system
Array of system gate names (same names as in the
.Va claims.system
configuration).
.Nm
mints a system access token with the specified gates and passes
it to the agent.
.El
.El
.Ss Examples
System agent managing core jid0 services:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/oracled.d/system.ucl
label = "system";
program = "/usr/libexec/oracled/agent";
provides = ["system"];
requires = ["ORACLED"];
restart = "always";
capabilities {
    paths = ["/var/log", "/var/run"];
    system = ["kldload", "kldunload", "kldstat",
              "reboot", "sysctl"];
}
# Agent reads /etc/oracled.d/system.d/ for:
#   syslogd.ucl, devd.ucl, sys-kldload.ucl, etc.
.Ed
.Pp
Network agent managing services in a jail:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/oracled.d/network.ucl
label = "network";
program = "/usr/libexec/oracled/agent";
provides = ["networking"];
requires = ["system"];
jail = "net";
restart = "always";
capabilities {
    paths = ["/etc/ssh", "/usr/local/etc/nginx"];
    network = [
        { port = 22; protocol = "tcp"; direction = "bind"; },
        { port = 80; protocol = "tcp"; direction = "bind"; },
        { port = 443; protocol = "tcp"; direction = "bind"; },
    ];
}
# Agent reads /etc/oracled.d/network.d/ for:
#   sshd.ucl, nginx.ucl, dhcpd.ucl
.Ed
.Pp
Container agent running an OCI workload:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/oracled.d/webapp.ucl
label = "webapp";
program = "/usr/libexec/oracled/agent";
requires = ["networking"];
jail = "webapp";
restart = "on-failure";
capabilities {
    network = [
        { port = 8080; protocol = "tcp"; direction = "bind"; },
    ];
}
# Agent reads /etc/oracled.d/webapp.d/ for
# container image config (OCI/Podman)
.Ed
.Ss Agent Environment
Each agent receives a minimal environment:
.Bl -tag -width "ORACLED_TOKEN_FDS"
.It Ev PATH
.Pa /sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
.It Ev ORACLED_PAIR_FD
File descriptor number for the cap_rt pair channel back to
.Nm
(always 3).
.It Ev ORACLED_TOKEN_FDS
Comma-separated list of file descriptor numbers for capability
tokens (starting at 4).
Each token must be authorized via
.Dv FI_OP_AUTHORIZE
or
.Dv SYS_OP_AUTHORIZE
after exec.
.It Ev ORACLED_LABEL
The agent label from the manifest.
The agent uses this to locate its service directory at
.Pa /etc/oracled.d/{label}.d/ .
.It Ev USER
Set if
.Va user
is specified in the manifest.
.It Ev HOME
Home directory of the agent user.
Only set when
.Va user
is specified in the manifest.
.El
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
.Nm
is instrumented with DTrace USDT probes in the
.Ql oracled
provider.
Probes are zero-cost when DTrace is not attached.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "claim-path-fail(path)" -compact
.It Li startup
Daemon initialization complete.
.It Li shutdown(reason)
Daemon shutting down.
Reason is a signal number or 0 for control socket shutdown.
.It Li config-load(path)
Configuration file parsed.
.It Li claim-path(path)
File or directory claimed successfully.
.It Li claim-path-fail(path)
File or directory claim failed.
.It Li claim-net(port, protocol)
Network endpoint claimed.
.It Li claim-net-fail(port, protocol)
Network endpoint claim failed.
.It Li integrity(flags)
Capprotect integrity shield activated.
.It Li ctl-accept(uid)
Control socket connection accepted.
.It Li ctl-cmd(op, uid)
Control command dispatched.
.It Li ctl-deny(op, uid)
Control command denied (EPERM).
.It Li svc-start(label, pid)
Service fork/exec'd.
.It Li svc-exec(label, pid)
Service exec confirmed via
.Dv NOTE_EXEC .
.It Li svc-exit(label, pid, status)
Service exited.
.It Li svc-restart(label, count)
Service restart scheduled.
.It Li svc-load(label)
New service manifest loaded at runtime.
.It Li reload()
Configuration reload initiated.
.It Li error(subsys, msg)
Notable error.
.El
.Pp
Example: trace all service lifecycle events:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtrace -n 'oracled*:::svc-*'
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/libexec/oracled/"
.It Pa /etc/oracled.conf
Configuration file (UCL format).
Optional.
.It Pa /etc/oracled.d/
Service manifest directory.
Each
.Pa *.ucl
file declares a service for
.Nm
to start, its dependencies, and the capability tokens it requires.
.It Pa /usr/libexec/oracled/
Default system service binaries.
These implement system operations (kldload, reboot, sysctl, etc.)
and are started by
.Nm
at boot.
Replaceable by providing alternate manifests in
.Pa /etc/oracled.d/ .
.It Pa /var/run/oracled.pid
Default pidfile.
.It Pa /var/run/oracled.sock
Default control socket (mode 0700).
.It Pa /dev/cap_rt
CAP_RT control device, isolated by
.Nm
when configured.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
.Nm
exits with an error if the configuration file contains syntax errors.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Minimal configuration with full integrity hardening:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
integrity {
    ptrace = true;
    signal = true;
    visible = true;
    wait = true;
    sched = true;
    core = true;
    ktrace = true;
}
.Ed
.Pp
Claim sensitive files and a privileged port:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
claims {
    paths = [
        "/dev/mem",
        "/boot/kernel",
    ];
    network = [
        { port = 443; protocol = "tcp"; direction = "bind"; },
    ];
}
.Ed
.Pp
Service manifest for a kernel module loader:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/oracled.d/sys-kldload.ucl
label = "sys-kldload";
program = "/usr/libexec/oracled/sys-kldload";
provides = ["kldload"];
requires = ["ORACLED"];
restart = "always";
capabilities {
    system = ["kldload", "kldunload", "kldstat"];
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr procctl 2 ,
.Xr cap_rt 4 ,
.Xr oraclectl 8 ,
.Xr rc 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in 5BSD as the initial Oracle userspace service manager.
